{"slug": "sichuan-celery-and-tofu-salad", "nyt_id": 1013219, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1013219-sichuan-celery-and-tofu-salad", "name": "Sichuan Celery and Tofu Salad", "author": "Mark Bittman", "description": "This otherwise simple salad, adapted from a dish at Szechuan Gourmet in Manhattan, may require a trip to a Chinese market for Szechuan peppercorns and pressed tofu. And you’ll need to make your own chili oil! But here’s the thing. The effort, which really isn’t much more than difficult shopping, produces an elegant dish that is worth whatever shoe leather or mileage you’ll expend getting the ingredients together. It is astonishingly good. And we’ll even allow one substitution. You can use regular celery instead of the Chinese version! Mark Bittman", "servings": {"text": "4 servings", "min": 4, "max": 4, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 20, "raw": {"total": "PT20M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 cup peanut oil", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "cup", "item": "peanut oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "1 piece ginger, 2 inches long, cut into 3 or 4 slices", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "piece", "item": "ginger", "preparation": "2 inches long, cut into 3 or 4 slices", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 piece cinnamon, 3 inches long", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "piece", "item": "cinnamon", "preparation": "3 inches long", "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "3 cloves", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "clove", "item": "3 cloves", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "1 teaspoon coriander seeds", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tsp", "item": "coriander seeds", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "1 teaspoon cumin seeds", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tsp", "item": "cumin seeds", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "4 star anise", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "unit": null, "item": "star anise", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "1 tablespoon Sichuan peppercorns", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "sichuan peppercorns", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "¼ cup red chile flakes, or more to taste", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "unit": "cup", "item": "red chile flakes", "preparation": "or more to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 10, "raw": "Salt", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 11, "raw": "1 bunch Chinese celery, or regular celery, thinly sliced", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "bunch", "item": "chinese celery", "preparation": "or regular celery, thinly sliced", "optional": false}, {"position": 12, "raw": "4 to 6 ounces pressed tofu, thinly sliced or julienned", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "unit": "oz", "item": "pressed tofu", "preparation": "thinly sliced or julienned", "optional": false, "quantity_max": 6.0}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Heat oil in a small saucepan over medium-high heat; when it simmers, add ginger, cinnamon, cloves, coriander, cumin, star anise, Sichuan peppercorns and chili flakes. Remove from heat and let sit until cool, at least 1 hour and preferably 2 or 3. Strain oil into a bowl or jar and discard spices."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Put a pot of salted water over high heat and bring to a boil. Add celery and blanch for about a minute; drain in colander and run under cold water to stop the cooking process."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Layer celery and tofu on serving plates, drizzle with chili oil and sprinkle with salt."}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 546.5, "protein_g": 4.9, "carbohydrates_g": 9.0, "fat_g": 57.1, "saturated_fat_g": 9.7, "unsaturated_fat_g": 18.5, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 334.8, "fiber_g": 3.8, "sugar_g": 1.4, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": ["asian-chinese"], "diet": [], "course": ["salad"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["quick", "tofu"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2014/03/05/dining/tofusalad-still/tofusalad-still-superJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 96}, "video_url": "https://vp.nyt.com/video/2010/01/01/7191_1_celeryandtofu_wg_480p.mp4", "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2010-08-04", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T22:35:48Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "simple-chickpea-soup", "nyt_id": 1014716, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1014716-simple-chickpea-soup", "name": "Simple Chickpea Soup", "author": "Emily Weinstein", "description": "This recipe came to The Times in 2013, when the food writers Michael Pollan and Michael Moss were prompted to make “a tasty, reasonably healthy lunch” using ingredients available at most grocery stores. “No farmers’ market produce, no grass-fed beef or artisanal anything,” the prompt stated. They came up with a few simple dishes: pizza, a salad of sliced avocados and oranges, and this simple but flavorful soup, which Mr. Pollan regularly made for his family and relies on canned garbanzos. Emily Weinstein", "servings": {"text": "6 servings", "min": 6, "max": 6, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 75, "raw": {"total": "PT1H15M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 large yellow onion, finely diced", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": null, "item": "large yellow onion", "preparation": "finely diced", "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "3 tablespoons olive oil, more for serving", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "olive oil", "preparation": "more for serving", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 clove garlic, slivered", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "clove", "item": "garlic", "preparation": "slivered", "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "2 (15 ½-ounce) cans chickpeas", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "unit": "can", "item": "chickpeas", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "Juice of ½ lemon, more to taste", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "juice of ½ lemon", "preparation": "more to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "Salt and pepper", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt and pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "Parmesan, optional", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "parmesan", "preparation": "optional", "optional": true}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "In a soup pot over medium heat, sauté onion in olive oil until onion is translucent and has lost its bite. Add garlic and cook until slightly colored."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Rinse chickpeas in cold water, then add to pot. Add water to pot until chickpeas are covered to the depth of 1 inch."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Cook over medium heat (soup should bubble, but not come to a full boil) for an hour, or until the chickpeas are soft and silky. Add lemon juice and season with salt and pepper. Serve topped with grated Parmesan or a drizzle of good olive oil if desired."}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 277.3333333333333, "protein_g": 10.8, "carbohydrates_g": 36.5, "fat_g": 10.9, "saturated_fat_g": 1.3, "unsaturated_fat_g": 2.1, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 433.2, "fiber_g": 10.1, "sugar_g": 7.1, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": ["appetizer-dinner-soup"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["budget", "chickpea", "great-leftovers", "lemon", "one-pot", "onion"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2025/09/02/multimedia/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2025/09/02/multimedia/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml-googleFourByThree.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2025/09/02/multimedia/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2025/09/02/multimedia/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml/Simple-Chickpea-Soup-vwml-facebookJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 814}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2013-05-01", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:54:17Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "simple-provencal-winter-squash-gratin", "nyt_id": 1015468, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1015468-simple-provencal-winter-squash-gratin", "name": "Simple Provençal Winter Squash Gratin", "author": "Tara Parker-Pope", "description": "I’ve offered a few winter squash gratin recipes over the years, but none as simple as this one. There’s little more than squash here, seasoned with lots of garlic and fresh herbs. Dicing all the squash takes time, but then the work is just about done. If you want to use a food processor, you can, but you can’t get even pieces that way. The recipe is based on one of my favorite recipes in Richard Olney’s book “Simple French Food.”", "servings": {"text": "6 servings", "min": 6, "max": 6, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 120, "raw": {"total": "PT2H"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "3 pounds winter squash (like 1 large butternut), peeled, seeds and membranes removed, cut in ¼-inch to ½-inch dice", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "lb", "item": "winter squash", "preparation": "peeled, seeds and membranes removed, cut in ¼-inch to ½-inch dice", "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "4 garlic cloves, minced", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "unit": null, "item": "garlic cloves", "preparation": "minced", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "¼ cup bread crumbs", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "unit": "cup", "item": "bread crumbs", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "½ cup finely chopped fresh parsley", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "cup", "item": "finely chopped fresh parsley", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "1 tablespoon fresh thyme leaves", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "fresh thyme leaves", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "Salt and freshly ground pepper", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt and freshly ground pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "3 tablespoons extra virgin olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "extra virgin olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "None"}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 161.5, "protein_g": 3.1, "carbohydrates_g": 24.3, "fat_g": 7.4, "saturated_fat_g": 1.1, "unsaturated_fat_g": 0.9, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 572.5, "fiber_g": 4.1, "sugar_g": 5.3, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": [], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": []}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2011/11/02/science/02recipehealth/02recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2011/11/02/science/02recipehealth/02recipehealth-facebookJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 182}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2011-11-30", "scraped_at": "2026-09-07T00:00:32Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "simple-seaweed-salad", "nyt_id": 1015648, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1015648-simple-seaweed-salad", "name": "Simple Seaweed Salad", "author": "Mark Bittman", "description": "The easiest way to produce this is to buy a small package of mixed seaweeds and proceed from there. But once you get more familiar with seaweed, you can use wakame, kelp, hijiki, or others, alone or in any combination you like.", "servings": {"text": "4 servings", "min": 4, "max": 4, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 20, "raw": {"total": "PT20M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 ounce assorted dried seaweeds or wakame", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "oz", "item": "assorted dried seaweeds or wakame", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "¼ cup minced shallot, scallion, or red onion", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "unit": "cup", "item": "minced shallot", "preparation": "scallion, or red onion", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "2 tablespoons soy sauce, or to taste", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "soy sauce", "preparation": "or to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "1 tablespoon rice wine or other light vinegar, or to taste", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "rice wine or other light vinegar", "preparation": "or to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "1 tablespoon mirin or 1 teaspoon sugar, or to taste", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "mirin or 1 teaspoon sugar", "preparation": "or to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "½ tablespoon dark sesame oil, or to taste", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "dark sesame oil", "preparation": "or to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "Pinch cayenne, or to taste", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "pinch cayenne", "preparation": "or to taste", "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "Salt, if necessary", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt", "preparation": "if necessary", "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "1 tablespoon toasted sesame seeds, optional", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "toasted sesame seeds", "preparation": "optional", "optional": true}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Rinse the seaweed once and soak it in at least 10 times its volume of water. When tender, about 5 minutes later, drain and gently squeeze the mixture to remove excess water. Pick through the seaweed to sort out any hard bits (there may be none) and chop or cut up (you may find it easier to use scissors) if the pieces are large. Put in a bowl."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Toss with the shallot, soy sauce, vinegar, mirin, sesame oil, cayenne, and salt. Taste and add salt or other seasonings as necessary. Serve garnished with the sesame seeds."}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 44.25, "protein_g": 1.6, "carbohydrates_g": 2.4, "fat_g": 2.9, "saturated_fat_g": 0.4, "unsaturated_fat_g": 1.2, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 443.4, "fiber_g": 0.5, "sugar_g": 0.5, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": ["japanese"], "diet": [], "course": ["dinner-lunch-salad"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["quick"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/assets/recipe-wallpaper/wallpaper-1200x675.png", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/assets/recipe-wallpaper/wallpaper-1200x900.png", "alt": "", "primary": false}, {"url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/assets/recipe-wallpaper/wallpaper-1200x1200.png", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 53}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2008-02-11", "scraped_at": "2026-09-07T00:02:18Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
